## What this predicate means

**A `has-domain` B** — A's source-type (domain) is B.  Applied to predicate memorials: the predicate's domain is the class of entity that can SERVE AS the subject of the relation.

Examples:
- `:mm.memory/cites has-domain :mm/Memory` (citation source must be a memorial)
- `:mm.memory/created-by has-domain :mm/Memory` (creation is attributed to memorial subjects)
- `:mm.memory/specializes has-domain :dt/Property` (sub-property is a property-on-property)

## Substrate identity

- **Slot ident**: `{{predicate-ident}}`
- **Domain**: `{{domain}}` (typically `:dt/Property`)
- **Range**: `{{range}}` (typically `:dt/Class`)
- **Cardinality**: `{{cardinality}}` (typically `:many` — a predicate may have multiple domain classes via disjunction)
- **Sub-property of**: `:dt/property-domain-relation`

## Formal signature

`{{predicate-name}} : {{domain}} × {{range}} → Bool`

## When to use

Declare `has-domain:` when:
- A is a predicate / property definition
- B is the class restricting which entities can serve as subjects of A
- Audit invariants on conformance can verify (source-of-edge ∈ domain)

## Audit invariants

- "predicate: domain/range conformance" — for every fact `x A y`, `x` must be an instance of the declared domain of A (or its subclass).  RDFS rule rdfs2.

Cross-check via `/memory-audit`.

## Formal ontology mappings

Aligned predicates from established formal vocabularies:

- **RDFS** `rdfs:domain` — exact semantic match (the canonical domain-restriction predicate; entailment via rdfs2)
- **OWL** `rdfs:domain` — same (OWL uses RDFS's domain predicate directly)
- **SHACL** `sh:targetClass` — close analogue (shape-validation context, slightly different semantics)
- **DC Terms** — no direct equivalent
- **PROV-O** — no direct equivalent
- **SKOS** — no direct equivalent

